dn't like your tactics  at all and in fact the judgment you  received in ecuador i guess has been  upended in this country  well look this these are the facts i'm  sure i disagree with chevron's view  obviously we've been at loggerheads for  years um you know we meaning me and  other group other lawyers on behalf of  these indigenous peoples won a 10  billion dollar pollution judgment after  an eight-year trial that has been  affirmed by ecuador supreme court it's  been a firm for enforcement purposes by  canada's supreme court and all 28  appellate judges there's one judge in  the u.s that they always cite judge  kaplan who ruled against me after a  non-jury trial where they paid a witness  to claim i bribed a judge it didn't  happen it's been rejected by 28  appellate judges in other countries it  did not happen definitely did not happen  no it didn't there was no bribery of a  judge  they paid a witness to say that about me  but it did not happen there's no  corroborating evidence  judges serious judges all over the world  have looked at this and rejected it  so you know it's just not true  now it is a judicial ruling here by this  one judge and they try to play on it  constantly when they give statements to  journalists but it's simply not true the  judgment is legitimate it's been  affirmed by six different appellate  courts and it's owned by the it's not my  judgment it's the judgment of the people  of ecuador they have lawyers and their  intention as i understand it is to make  sure that judgment is enforced so the  money is paid and chevron is forced to  comply with the rule of law  and finally just to uh how are you doing  now out of prison back with your family  for the holidays how are you doing  personally i'm um  thrilled  to be home  um i i can't explain it i i've been  swirling with emotion you know since i  got out  um i've been  i felt i i realized how much pain i  carried out of that prison i i mean the  experiences to see so many people hurt  inside and to come out see my family was  hurting  and i'll say this you know  going into prison is often is harder on  the people who love you on the outside  than it is on you on the inside i've  realized that a lot of people told me  that it's really true  you know i could at least control to an  extent what i did inside you know but  when you're outside you're like oh my  god what's going on is he you know  there's no communication you know is he  even sleeping and i can tell you i  didn't have one decent night's sleep in  45 days it was it was  really unsettling  i do want to acknowledge though that  it's you know i met a lot of strong  people in prison who who you know i  learned from them how they handled the  time and my sentence obviously was  ridiculously short compared to most  people  um but to me it felt like an eternity  i'm i'm really happy to be home and i'm  happy to be with my family and i'm happy  to be able to  speak again to people like you so thank  you for asking  all right stephen danzano thank you so  much for being with us again be here  tonight  thank you  up next inside the proud boys and the  oath keepers in america's other violent  extremist groups will be joined by the  photojournalist who has been tracking  them you're watching brian ross  investigates on the law and crime trial  network tonight a media shout out for  the investigative photojournalist who's  made it his mission to track the  emergence of america's violent extremist  groups a dangerous job but that's just  what zach roberts has been doing again  and again zach thanks for joining us  tonight these are crazy people and your  family must think you're crazy to be  dealing with them  uh yes uh it's taken uh  many years and i'm still not quite sure  if my uh like my mother understands  exactly what i do or more specifically  why i do it because she knows that  um it's not financially benefiting like  just journalism in general as we all  know is not exactly the greatest you  know financial move  when you're inside these groups and  dealing with them do they come across to  you as having intelligence are they  scheming what what gives with these  groups  i mean most i mean it really depends on  kind of which group you're talking about  but like the groups you know we were  talking about because of this uh lawsuit  today um you know the oath keepers and  the proud boys it's a full range of guys  who just want to hang out with other  men and and get into fights and also  commit a little bit of racism now now  and then um but um but yeah no i mean i  don't think that usually there's that  many great schemers um you know outside  of patriot front which is the group that  you're seeing they they definitely do a  lot of planning sessions um and they're  very particular about the clothing that  they wear the flags that they think how  they look you know and they're they're  they're kind of uh the way that they  look to the rest of society and you know  really big thing is it's looking scary  um the proud boys while they're a  violent um  western chauvinist gang um who has  committed multiple  dozens of acts of violence across the  country and have really never really  been held to task outside of a handful  of people in new york city and a couple  other places um  they they're not planning for anything  big usually um but as we can see in this  lawsuit  that the attorney general of d.c just  dropped  uh they they planned and they conspired  with um with uh with each other to  commit a lot of the violence uh that you  saw on january 6.  we know they're disdain for the media  how do they treat you we've just got 30  seconds here  again i mean it fully arranges some some  people they really want their photos  taken um and they play into the cameras  and other people you get physical  threats um i've had colleagues of mine  who have had equipment destroyed been  attacked um by by all these groups and  uh and so every single time you talk to  somebody 